gers. If $m∣l,$ $n∣l,$ and $\\text{gcd}(m,n)=1$ then\n\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "For any integers $m,$ $n,$ and $l,$ we have that\n\n$$\nm∣l,\\,n∣l,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\text{gcd}(m,n)=1\\,⟹\\,mn∣l.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fccf0191-c95e-52de-8562-9ed3ab37548e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fccf0191-c95e-52de-8562-9ed3ab37548e" :answer-field/id #uuid "fccf0191-c95e-52de-8562-9ed3ab37548e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-dd943c8e-54fe-5ba7-a8ed-cc0bdbf78bd0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-565e3687-0b22-5dd4-b459-4bf65f659410" "answer-35de53fc-7740-5f42-aef3-a33cd4fdcce8" "answer-cfaf96d7-96d9-501c-bdcd-0972c79cd81a" "answer-dd943c8e-54fe-5ba7-a8ed-cc0bdbf78bd0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-565e3687-0b22-5dd4-b459-4bf65f659410" :answer/id #uuid "565e3687-0b22-5dd4-b459-4bf65f659410" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(m+n)∣l" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-35de53fc-7740-5f42-aef3-a33cd4fdcce8" :answer/id #uuid "35de53fc-7740-5f42-aef3-a33cd4fdcce8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "l∣(m+n)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cfaf96d7-96d9-501c-bdcd-0972c79cd81a" :answer/id #uuid "cfaf96d7-96d9-501c-bdcd-0972c79cd81a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "l∣mn" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dd943c8e-54fe-5ba7-a8ed-cc0bdbf78bd0" :answer/id #uuid "dd943c8e-54fe-5ba7-a8ed-cc0bdbf78bd0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "mn∣l" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198890 :question/problem "Let $d,h,$ and $f$ be integers. If $d∣f,$ $h∣f,$ and $\\text{gcd}(d,h)=1$ then\n\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "For any integers $d,$ $h,$ and $f,$ we have that\n\n$$\nd∣f,\\,h∣f,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\text{gcd}(d,h)=1\\,⟹\\,dh∣f.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186196773 :answer-field/correct 17592186196777 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-237e8d15-d348-5681-a8d5-20db7f138f56" :question/id #uuid "237e8d15-d348-5681-a8d5-20db7f138f56" :question/math-academy-id "q-221464" :question/problem "Let $a,b,$ and $c$ be integers. If $a∣c,$ $b∣c,$ and $\\text{gcd}(a,b)=1$ then\n\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "For any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$ we have that\n\n$$\na∣c,\\,b∣c,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\text{gcd}(a,b)=1\\,⟹\\,ab∣c.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9b2776b2-4ae4-50ef-8d34-171dd3cff6ba"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9b2776b2-4ae4-50ef-8d34-171dd3cff6ba" :answer-field/id #uuid "9b2776b2-4ae4-50ef-8d34-171dd3cff6ba" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-6137f61b-fd1a-58a4-8b69-1b4894ee8d68" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6137f61b-fd1a-58a4-8b69-1b4894ee8d68" "answer-4ba3500e-7378-5987-8d40-8972a2087e81" "answer-29b4ecfe-b0b5-5ad3-aa03-585d63c93876" "answer-5f41de71-61ff-560d-ad22-239f79e21d2d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6137f61b-fd1a-58a4-8b69-1b4894ee8d68" :answer/id #uuid "6137f61b-fd1a-58a4-8b69-1b4894ee8d68" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "ab∣c" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4ba3500e-7378-5987-8d40-8972a2087e81" :answer/id #uuid "4ba3500e-7378-5987-8d40-8972a2087e81" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "c∣a" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-29b4ecfe-b0b5-5ad3-aa03-585d63c93876" :answer/id #uuid "29b4ecfe-b0b5-5ad3-aa03-585d63c93876" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "c∣ab" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5f41de71-61ff-560d-ad22-239f79e21d2d" :answer/id #uuid "5f41de71-61ff-560d-ad22-239f79e21d2d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "c∣b" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186199581 :tutorial/content "Recall that if $a$ and $b$ are integers where $a \\neq 0,$ the notation \n\n$$\na \\mid b\n$$\n\nmeans that $a$ divides $b.$ This means there exists an integer $k$ such that\n\n$$\nb = a \\cdot k.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, \n$a \\not\\mid b$\ndenotes the fact that $a$ does not divide $b.$\n\nThis simple definition can be used to prove some basic properties of integer divisibility." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186199586 :tutorial/content "The first property we'll study is called the **transitivity of divisibility.** It states the following:\n\n**Theorem**\n\n*For integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ with $a, b\\neq 0,$ we have*\n\n$\\qquad$ $a \\mid b \\quad$ *and* $\\quad b \\mid c$ $\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad$ $a \\mid c.$\n\nWe can prove this statement using the definition of divisibility.\n\n**Proof**\n\n*Given that $a \\mid b,$ there exists an integer $k_1$ such that*\n\n$$\nb=ak_1.\n$$\n\n*Similarly, given that $b \\mid c,$ there exists an integer $k_2$ such that*\n\n$$\nc=bk_2.\n$$\n\n*Substituting the expression for $b$ into our equation for $c,$ we get*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =b{k}_{2} \\\\  & =(a{k}_{1}){k}_{2} \\\\  & =a({k}_{1}{k}_{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Since $k_1$ and $k_2$ are integers, their product $k=k_1k_2$ is also an integer. Therefore,* \n\n$$\nc = ak\n$$\n\n*which, by the definition of divisibility, means that $a \\mid c.$*" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186199582 :tutorial/content "Let's now state our next property, known as the **divisibility of a product:** \n\n**Theorem**\n\n*For any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$*\n\n$\\qquad$ $c \\mid ab \\quad$ *and* $\\quad \\text{gcd}(a,c)=1$ $\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad$ $c \\mid b$\n\nThis statement assumes that $\\text{gcd}(a,c)=1$ (i.e., that $a$ and $c$ are coprime). This suggests we may want to use *Bézout’s identity* to prove this statement since it relates two integers to their greatest common divisor. \n\nRecall that Bézout’s identity states that for any two integers $a$ and $b,$ there exist integers $u$ and $v$ such that \n\n$$\nau+bv= \\textrm{gcd}(a,b)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\textrm{gcd}(a,b)$ is the greatest common divisor of $a$ and $b.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's prove this statement.\n\n**Proof**\n\n*Given that $\\text{gcd}(a,c)=1,$ by Bézout’s identity there exist integers $u$ and $v$ such that*\n\n$$\nau + cv = 1.\n$$\n\n*On the other hand, since $c\\: | \\: ab,$ there exists an integer $k$ such that*\n\n$$\nab=ck.\n$$\n\n*Multiplying both sides of the first equation by $b$ and substituting $ab=ck,$ we get the following:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}au+cv & =1 \\\\ (au+cv)b & =b \\\\ (ab)u+cvb & =b \\\\ (ck)u+cvb & =b \\\\ c(ku+vb) & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*As $k,u,v,$ and $b$ are integers, then $ku+ vb = m$ is an integer too. Hence,*\n\n$$\ncm = b\n$$\n\n*which implies that*\n\n$$\nc\\: | \\: b.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186199583 :tutorial/content "Let's now state our final property, known as the **divisibility of an integer by a product:**\n\n**Theorem**\n\n*For any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$*\n\n$\\qquad$ *$a \\:|\\: c,$ $\\quad b \\:|\\: c, \\quad$ and $\\quad \\text{gcd}(a,b)=1$ $\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad$ $ab \\:|\\: c.$*\n\nThe proof is given below.\n\n**Proof**\n\n*Given that $\\text{gcd}(a,b)=1,$ there exist integers $u$ and $v$ such that*\n\n$$\nau + bv = 1.\n$$\n\n*On the other hand, since $a\\: | \\: c$ and $b\\: | \\: c,$ there exist integers $k$ and $l$ such that*\n\n$$\nc=ak,\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\nc=bl.\n$$\n\n*Multiplying both sides of the first equation by $c$ and substituting $c=ak$ and $c=bl,$ we get the following:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}au+bv & =1 \\\\ (au+bv)c & =c \\\\ auc+bvc & =c \\\\ au(bl)+bv(ak) & =c \\\\ ab(ul+vk) & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*As $k,l,u,$ and $v$ are integers, then $ul + vk = m$ is an integer too. Hence,*\n\n$$\nab\\cdot m = c\n$$\n\n*which implies that*\n\n$$\nab\\: | \\: c.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198548 :activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
